  "commonMistakes": {
    "title": "Common Pitfalls & Mistakes in US Federal Income Tax Filing",
    "html": "<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-3\">\n  <li><strong>The Tax Bracket Fallacy:</strong> Many people believe that getting a raise that bumps them into a higher tax bracket (e.g. from 12% to 22%) will reduce their total take-home pay. This is mathematically impossible in a progressive bracket system: <em>only the dollars earned above the threshold</em> are taxed at 22%!</li>\n  <li><strong>Confusing Tax Credits with Tax Deductions:</strong> A $2,000 deduction reduces taxable income, saving you $440 if you are in the 22% bracket. A $2,000 tax credit wipes out $2,000 of tax liability dollar-for-dollar. Always prioritize tax credits!</li>\n  <li><strong>Itemizing When the Standard Deduction Is Higher:</strong> Since the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act doubled the standard deduction, over 88% of filers receive a bigger tax break by simply taking the standard deduction rather than spending hours gathering receipts for Schedule A.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ignoring State Income Tax Differences:</strong> Federal income tax is identical across all 50 states, but state income tax varies from 0% (in Texas, Florida, Washington, Nevada, Wyoming, South Dakota, Alaska, Tennessee, and New Hampshire) up to 13.3% in California.</li>\n  <li><strong>Underestimating FICA Payroll Taxes:</strong> In addition to federal income tax, employees pay 7.65% in FICA taxes (6.2% Social Security up to the wage base cap plus 1.45% Medicare with no cap). Self-employed individuals pay the full 15.3% SE tax.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
