  "limitations": {
    "title": "Statutory Scope & Limitations",
    "html": "<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2\">\n  <li><strong>State &amp; Municipal Income Tax:</strong> Computes federal income tax. State income taxes must be calculated using our dedicated State Tax Calculator.</li>\n  <li><strong>Alternative Minimum Tax (AMT):</strong> High-income filers with extensive incentive stock options (ISOs) or private activity bonds may trigger AMT calculations under Form 6251.</li>\n  <li><strong>Net Investment Income Tax (NIIT):</strong> High earners with investment income exceeding $200k (Single) or $250k (MFJ) are subject to a 3.8% NIIT surcharge.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "question": "What are the federal income tax brackets and rates for 2025/2026?",
      "answer": "The federal income tax rates are 10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35%, and 37%. Each rate applies only to income within that specific bracket range after subtracting the standard deduction ($15,000 for single filers, $30,000 for married filing jointly in 2025/2026)."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the difference between marginal tax rate and effective tax rate?",
      "answer": "Your marginal tax rate is the highest bracket applied to your last dollar of income (e.g. 22%). Your effective tax rate is the actual percentage of your total gross income paid to the IRS (e.g. 12.1%), which is always lower because lower dollars are taxed at 0%, 10%, and 12%."
    },
    {
      "question": "How much is the standard deduction for 2025/2026 by filing status?",
      "answer": "For the 2025/2026 tax year, the standard deduction is $15,000 for Single filers, $30,000 for Married Filing Jointly, $22,500 for Head of Household, and $15,000 for Married Filing Separately. Taxpayers age 65 or older receive an additional $1,600 to $2,000 deduction."
    },
    {
      "question": "Should I take the standard deduction or itemize deductions on Schedule A?",
      "answer": "You should itemize only if your total qualifying deductions (mortgage interest, state and local taxes up to $10,000, medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI, and charitable gifts) exceed your standard deduction ($15,000 Single / $30,000 Married). Over 88% of Americans claim the standard deduction."
    },
    {
      "question": "Will moving into a higher tax bracket reduce my net take-home pay?",
      "answer": "No. The US tax system is progressive. Moving into a higher bracket only taxes the dollars that fall inside the new bracket at the higher rate. Every dollar earned below that threshold remains taxed at the lower rates, guaranteeing that a pay raise always increases your take-home pay."
    },
    {
      "question": "How do tax credits differ from tax deductions?",
      "answer": "Tax deductions reduce your taxable income before tax is calculated (saving you a percentage of the deduction equal to your tax bracket). Tax credits reduce your actual tax bill dollar-for-dollar. For example, a $2,000 Child Tax Credit reduces your final tax obligation directly by $2,000."
    }
  ],
